<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Buxar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1764">1764</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Buxar was fought in October 1764 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> army under Hector Munro and the combined forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-qasim">Mir Qasim</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shuja-ud-daula">Shuja-ud-Daula</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Awadh</a> and the Mughal emperor Shah Alam II. The British victory led to the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prayagraj">Allahabad</a> of 1765, by which the Company gained the diwani, the right to collect the revenue of Bengal, Bihar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Orissa</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-buxar.jpg" alt="Battle of Buxar" width="320" height="154" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: David Martin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_Hector_Munro_by_David_Martin_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The battle was fought on 22–23 October 1764 at Buxar, a strong fortified town on the banks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>, about 130 kilometres west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patna">Patna</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> force under Major Hector Munro numbered 7,072, about 1,800 British regulars and 5,300 sepoys. Against it stood the combined armies of the Mughal emperor Shah Alam II and his allies <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-qasim">Mir Qasim</a>, Nawab of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, Maharaja <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balwant-singh-of-benares">Balwant Singh of Benares</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shuja-ud-daula">Shuja-ud-Daula</a>, Nawab of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Awadh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-vizier">Grand Vizier</a> to the emperor, estimated at over 40,000 men.</p><p>Mirza Najaf Khan commanded the right flank of the Mughal army and advanced first, at daybreak. The Company lines formed within twenty minutes and turned the advance back, and by midday the battle was over. As his forces withdrew, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shuja-ud-daula">Shuja-ud-Daula</a> blew up large tumbrils and three magazines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a>. Munro pursued him towards the Karmanasa River, and Shuja-ud-Daula destroyed the boat-bridge after crossing, abandoning the emperor and some of his own troops. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-qasim">Mir Qasim</a> fled and was exiled by Shuja-ud-Daula, while Najaf Khan rallied the remaining forces around the emperor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>John William Fortescue gave British casualties as 847 and those of the Indian allies as 2,000 dead; another source counts 69 European and 664 sepoy casualties and 6,000 on the Mughal side. The victors captured 133 guns and over a million <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupees</a>. The emperor chose to negotiate with the British, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shuja-ud-daula">Shuja-ud-Daula</a>, pursued westward, eventually surrendered. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-qasim">Mir Qasim</a> carried away gemstones worth 3 million rupees but died in poverty in 1777.</p><p>The defeated rulers signed the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prayagraj">Allahabad</a> in 1765, which granted the Company the diwani, the right to collect revenue from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Orissa</a> on behalf of the Mughal emperor. In 1772 the Company abolished local rule and took complete control of the province of Bengal-Bihar. The battle is seen as the beginning of the end of Mughal political control, and as exposing the lack of unity and coordination among the Nawabs and the emperor, which made it easier for the British to defeat them.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Buxar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The turning point came at the <b>Battle of Buxar</b> in 1764, when the East India Company defeated the alliance of Shuja-ud-Daula and the deposed nawab of Bengal, Mir Kasim.</blockquote><a href="/empire/awadh-state">Awadh State · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…Clive's victory at the Battle of Plassey in 1757 deposed the Nawab of Bengal Siraj-ud-Daulah in favour of Mir Jafar, and the <b>Battle of Buxar</b> in 1764 forced the emperor Shah Alam II to appoint the Company diwan, or revenue collector, of Bengal, Bihar and Orissa in 1765.</blockquote><a href="/empire/company-rule-in-india">Company rule in India · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The rising power of the English East India Company, confirmed by its victories at Plassey in 1757 and <b>Buxar</b> in 1764, steadily eroded Mughal sovereignty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Battle of Buxar</b> the Company received the diwani in 1765, beginning Company rule in India; the nawabs survived as pensioners.</blockquote><a href="/state/nawabate-of-bengal">Nawabate of Bengal</a></li></ul></section>
